export const KNOWLEDGE = "<page url=\"https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/\" source=\"public/index.html\">\nSalesforce + AI · Sydney, Australia\nHemayet Hossain\nSalesforce Administrator and CRM support candidate in Sydney, seeking my first paid Salesforce or IT role. I practise through self-directed simulations and am building toward forward deployed engineering as a longer-term goal.\nGitHub (https://github.com/hossainconsulting) Personal LinkedIn (https://www.linkedin.com/in/hemayethossain/) Agency LinkedIn (https://www.linkedin.com/company/hossain-consulting) Agency Instagram (https://www.instagram.com/hossainconsulting/) hossainconsulting.com (https://hossainconsulting.com/) All links (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/links)\nWhat this is\nThese self-directed Salesforce simulations explore requirements, data modelling, security, automation and handover in fictional business settings. They are at different stages of development. Each repository's dated records distinguish completed work, planned scope and unresolved decisions.\nWhere available, the repositories include configuration, seed data and deliverables such as design documents, SOPs and analyses. Read these alongside dated test results and session notes to understand what was implemented, how it was checked and what remains unfinished.\nDisclosure\nThese are simulations, not client work. SunRise Solar Solutions, Meridian Field Services, TradeLink Group, Meridian Appliance Care, Coastline Retail Group, Ironbark Industrial Supply and Kurrajong Energy are fictional companies. Every scenario, stakeholder and record is invented for the purpose of developing and evidencing implementation skill.\nNo real customer data appears anywhere in these projects. I'd rather you know that in the first thirty seconds than find out later.\nFor recruiters, agencies and hiring managers\nThis portfolio supports Salesforce, automation and integration roles. The projects are self-directed simulations at different stages; dated records distinguish completed work, planned scope and unresolved decisions.\nWhat I'm open to\nSalesforce Administrator and CRM support employment or suitable contract opportunities, including agency placements. Forward deployed engineering is my longer-term direction. Business service enquiries go through Hossain Consulting (https://hossainconsulting.com/services), which is preparing to launch paid services.\nVerify me in ten minutes\nStart with the public User Lifecycle SOP (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/salesforce-user-lifecycle-sop) and the Salesforce credential record linked below. SunRise project evidence is private; email me to request a walkthrough or a suitable evidence extract.\nHow I document practice\nI use dated records, evidence where available, handover notes and explicit unresolved decisions. These are learning conventions, not a claim of completed client delivery.\nStraight answer on experience\nThe projects are documented simulations in my own orgs. I have not yet completed a paid client engagement, and I would rather you hear that from me than discover it.\nhemayet@hemayethossain.com · Personal LinkedIn (https://www.linkedin.com/in/hemayethossain/) · Email is the contact fallback; the assistant source still requires live configuration and verification.\nBuilding toward Forward Deployed Engineering\nLonger-term direction: Forward Deployed Engineer (FDE). The learning plan below connects ten domains to customer-facing engineering. It is planned scope, not a claim of proficiency or completed delivery.\nMy immediate job search remains focused on Salesforce Administrator and CRM support opportunities. Alongside that foundation, I am developing the ability to take a customer problem through discovery, prototype, integration, deployment and handover.\nLinux-first foundation\nPython, SQL, Git, HTTP and JSON; Linux administration with Bash, SSH and systemd; YAML configuration and Ansible automation. PowerShell on Linux is a secondary scripting skill. Windows administration is outside this learning plan.\nPractice environment\nThe six Linux VMs below provide the existing lab structure. Editor choice is flexible; VS Code and Vim are suitable options. Listed tools are learning priorities, not a verified inventory of installed software.\nSkills, tools and project practice across ten domains\nPlanned FDE learning map — existing project statuses remain authoritative.\nDomain | Skills to develop | Tools to practise | Proposed project contribution\nSoftware engineering | Python, API design, SQL, debugging and tests | FastAPI, Pydantic, PostgreSQL, pytest, Git | Build a validated customer-operations API with role-based access.\nInfrastructure engineering | Networking, cloud foundations and reproducible provisioning | Docker, one cloud platform, Terraform or OpenTofu | Provision the application environment from version-controlled configuration.\nAI engineering | Structured outputs, retrieval, tool calling and evaluation | Model APIs, PostgreSQL with pgvector, Python evaluation scripts | Extend Home Services AI with cited answers and quality, latency and cost measurements.\nConsulting | Discovery, requirements, scope, tradeoffs and customer handover | Documents, spreadsheets, diagrams.net | Produce a discovery brief, acceptance criteria, demo and implementation proposal.\nWeb development | HTML, CSS, JavaScript, accessibility and API integration | Browser developer tools, TypeScript and React after fundamentals | Build a responsive interface for enquiries and support requests.\nServer engineering | Linux services, SSH, permissions, logs, backups and recovery | Bash, systemd, YAML, Ansible, Nginx | Configure a Linux application server and demonstrate a restore onto a fresh VM.\nEthical hacking | Authorised assessment, access-control testing and remediation | OWASP guidance, ZAP, Nmap in the isolated lab | Assess the portfolio application, document findings, fix issues and retest.\nCRM administration | Data models, data quality, permissions, flows and reporting | Salesforce, Salesforce CLI, Flow and REST APIs | Connect an application to a Salesforce simulation and verify record access and data mapping.\nWorkflow automation | Webhooks, authentication, retries, duplicate prevention and approvals | Python, n8n, Postman | Route enquiries into the CRM with failure alerts and a recovery procedure.\nDigital marketing consulting | Customer journeys, lead qualification and funnel measurement | UTM conventions, spreadsheets, CRM dashboards; GA4 where appropriate | Use synthetic enquiry data to report qualified leads and follow-up performance.\nThree connected portfolio projects\nUse fictional business scenarios and synthetic data. Build on the existing Salesforce simulations, Home Services AI and Ansible learning track; their current status labels remain unchanged.\n1. Customer operations application\nProposed\nPython · FastAPI · PostgreSQL · web interface · Salesforce integration\nCapture and manage enquiries or support requests through an accessible interface and validated API. Add authentication, record-level permissions and a sandbox CRM integration.\nEvidence to produce: discovery brief, architecture diagram, API documentation, meaningful tests and a demo showing allowed and denied access.\n2. AI-assisted enquiry and support workflow\nProposed extension\nHome Services AI · retrieval · model evaluation · CRM automation\nExtract structured enquiry details, route records and answer from approved documents with citations. Include human review before consequential actions, permission-aware retrieval, retry handling and duplicate-event protection.\nEvidence to produce: evaluation cases and results, failure scenarios, workflow diagram, recovery instructions and measured latency and model cost.\n3. Linux deployment and customer handover\nProposed\nBash · YAML · Ansible · Docker · CI · monitoring · recovery\nDeploy the application on Linux using repeatable configuration. Add automated checks, service monitoring, backups and an authorised security assessment. Rebuild the environment and restore its data to verify the operating instructions.\nEvidence to produce: deployment configuration, actual rebuild and restore results, remediation and retest notes, operating-cost estimate, incident runbook and a customer handover guide.\nLearning and job-readiness milestones\nFoundation: Linux, Bash, Python, Git, SQL, networking and HTTP.\nApplication delivery: APIs, database design, frontend basics, authentication and tests.\nOperations: YAML, Ansible, Docker, CI, one cloud platform, monitoring and tested recovery.\nIntegrations and AI: CRM APIs, webhooks, workflow reliability, retrieval and evaluation.\nCustomer delivery: practise discovery, scoping, demonstrations and documentation throughout; finish with acceptance checks, training and outcome measurement.\nFDE portfolio goal: demonstrate a customer problem, an implemented solution, verified behaviour, deployment and a usable handover. Publish dated evidence as work is completed. Keep forecasts separate from measured outcomes and simulation work separate from paid experience.\nWhere each skill is applied\nReview the public evidence and dated status below. Private evidence is available for discussion on request. These examples do not establish current org state or a deployed service.\nCapability | Evidence to open | Status boundary\nSalesforce administration | SunRise status and evidence requests | Private project evidence; historical progress and limitations are described below.\nAgent design | Service-agent pattern library (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/service-agent-patterns) | Written analysis; implementation and compliance decisions remain separate.\nClaude API and tool use | Worker source (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/portfolio/blob/main/src/index.js) | Source present; live model, lead delivery and launch controls require verification.\nLinux and Ansible | Curriculum and starter playbooks (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/portfolio/tree/main/learning/ansible-linux) | Planned learning track; all twelve modules marked Not started.\nProjects\nSunRise Solar Solutions\nIn progress\nSalesforce Administrator\nTwelve weeks as the incoming administrator at a 47-person NSW solar installer whose org has run unmaintained for six months — 340 duplicate accounts, a lead assignment rule that stopped matching reality when territory boundaries moved, and a quarter that missed quota with no explanation anyone can produce.\nHistorical record, 7 September 2026: Week 4 opened. Earlier records include duplicate-pair assessments, a partial Contact merge and interim custodianship of 666 Opportunities. Held merge decisions, access dependencies and other carry-forward work remain. This is a dated historical summary, not the latest project status or a live org verification. Current evidence is maintained in the private salesforce-sunrise-solar repository. Request a walkthrough to discuss completed work, open decisions and subsequent changes.\n12-week planned scope · Private simulation evidence Request current evidence & a walkthrough →\nMeridian Field Services\nQueued\nPlatform App Builder\nAn end-to-end implementation for a Sydney plumbing, electrical and HVAC company running its business on spreadsheets, a shared inbox and a group text thread. Eighteen phases, every one traced to a numbered requirement in the client's own words.\n18 phases repository → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/meridian-field-services)\nTradeLink Group\nQueued\nAdvanced Administrator · Sales Cloud · Service Cloud\nEight sprints inside a national home-services franchise network — 450 staff, three brands, six years of organic growth in the org. A security model reset, an acquisition data migration with a rehearsed rollback, an SLA machine for a contact centre at 40% volume growth, and a packaged go-live.\n8 sprints repository → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/tradelink-group)\nMeridian Appliance Care\nQueued\nAgentforce Specialist\nA six-week consulting engagement for a national warranty administrator handling 12,400 cases a month with zero self-service deflection. Agentforce was licensed after a board directive and never switched on. Includes a live incident with two seeded root causes and a blameless post-incident review.\n6 weeks repository → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/agentforce-meridian-care) pattern library → (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/service-agent-patterns)\nCoastline Retail Group\nQueued\nData Cloud Consultant\nTen weeks as the incoming data specialist at a 62-store NSW and Victorian homewares retailer whose customers exist three times over — once in the point of sale, once in e-commerce, once in the loyalty app. Identity resolution, calculated insights and activation, for a business that cannot currently answer how many customers it has.\n10 weeks repository → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/coastline-retail-group)\nIronbark Industrial Supply\nQueued\nSales Cloud Consultant\nA national industrial parts distributor with 140 reps across five states, forecasting out of spreadsheets, no territory model, and an opportunity stage set nobody agrees on. Territory management, forecasting and quoting rebuilt from discovery rather than from a feature list — plus Agentforce for Sales, licensed and never switched on.\n10 sprints repository → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/ironbark-industrial-supply)\nKurrajong Energy\nQueued\nService Cloud Consultant\nAn energy retailer with 340,000 residential customers. Complaints arrive by phone, by email, and through a web form that lands in a shared inbox. No knowledge base, ombudsman escalations tracked in a spreadsheet, and regulated response deadlines being missed — which gives entitlements and milestones a consequence beyond a red dashboard.\n10 sprints repository → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/kurrajong-energy)\nAI engineering\nPlanned tools for trades and home-services workflows, complementing the Salesforce projects above.\nHome Services AI\nPlanning / scaffold\nSelf-directed AI engineering learning project\nFive proposed tools: Quote Triage for structuring enquiries and identifying missing details; Notes to Invoice for drafting invoices using a rates table and calculations in code; Jobs MCP Server for database access and validated bookings; After-Hours Agent for enquiry triage and human escalation; and Eval Harness for testing quality, cost and regressions.\nThe five trades tools remain planned scaffolds. Content DNA Studio has application source and tests; its README reports historical mock-mode validation. Live API behavior and measured outcomes remain unverified.\nPlanned: 10 weeks · 5 tools repository → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/home-services-ai)\nStudy Prompt Builder\nFree tool\nPlain HTML and JavaScript · no backend, no tracking\nSeventeen tutoring and course-design prompts with the blanks turned into a form. Pick a template, fill in the subject, level and goal, and copy a finished prompt into Claude or any assistant. Answers stay in the browser.\n17 templates open the tool → (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/study/) the prompts → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/portfolio/blob/main/learning-course-templates.md)\nHome lab\nSix Linux virtual machines on my laptop, one per skill area, each running its own practice projects.\nI don't have paid IT experience yet, so I built a place to get hands-on practice. Each VM runs in VirtualBox, is reached over SSH with key-based login, and has a dated record of what was set up and how it was checked. Much of the setup was done with AI-assisted tooling (Claude Code and Codex) under my direction. Those records live in a private repository; I'm happy to walk through them on request.\nsalesforce-dev\nActive\nUbuntu 24.04 LTS · Salesforce\nWorking copies of the Salesforce simulations above, including SunRise Solar, developed against Developer Edition orgs with the Salesforce CLI. This is the lab for my job-search Salesforce projects.\npaperclip-dev\nActive\nFedora Server 44 · AI agent orchestration\nFresh Fedora Server install running Paperclip, an agent orchestration platform, as a systemd service. An agent is configured and has passed an environment check; it is paused and has not run business tasks. It is the intended home for running the business side, meaning CEO administration and winning clients. This portfolio site's source and Home Services AI are also maintained from this lab.\nagency-dev\nSet up\nDebian 12 · Client delivery\nWorkspace for delivering client work, with its own scoped GitHub deploy key. No client project is published from it yet.\nrhce-dev\nSet up\nRed Hat Enterprise Linux 9 · Linux administration, studying for RHCE\nRed Hat Linux administration projects and practice towards the Red Hat Certified Engineer exam. I don't hold the certification; exercises are not published yet.\nfde-dev\nSet up\nCentOS Stream 9 · Forward deployed engineering practice\nPractice environment for Python, APIs, integrations and Linux deployment (Bash, PowerShell 7, Ansible), working toward forward deployed engineering. No projects are published from it yet.\noscp-dev\nSet up\nKali Linux · Offensive Security practice, authorised lab only\nOffensive Security (OSCP) study and Kali administration projects, used only against systems I own or am authorised to test. I don't hold the certification; no work is published yet.\nInfrastructure\nWhat everything above runs on. A learning track rather than a simulated engagement: Linux by hand first, then Ansible to make it repeatable.\nAnsible with Linux\nPlanned\nLinux administration · Ansible\nA planned twelve-module curriculum across a four-VM lab — two Ubuntu web hosts and a Rocky Linux database host, so every playbook has to cope with two package managers. Users, permissions, systemd and SSH hardening done manually before any of it is automated; then inventory, idempotent playbooks, templates and handlers, roles, Vault, and a full lab rebuilt from a blank VM in one command. The planned modules will each end with a note recording what broke, not what the documentation says.\n12 modules · 4 hosts learning track → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/portfolio/tree/main/learning/ansible-linux)\nWriting\nOccasional notes on the tooling. Short, practical, and only published once I have actually used the thing.\nThe service-agent pattern library\nAgentforce · agent design\nTwo hundred \"agent ideas\" for service businesses collapse into about twenty-five patterns re-skinned across eight verticals. Each pattern gets a risk tier, an integration surface and an Australian compliance note, and the tier decides whether the model may generate the answer or must retrieve it.\n25 patterns · 3 tiers read → (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/service-agent-patterns)\nHow to use Claude with Microsoft 365\nAI in practice\nSeven places Claude earns its keep inside the Microsoft 365 apps most offices already run on — Outlook, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, OneNote, Teams and OneDrive — with a worked example for each, and an honest note on which parts need a connector and which are just paste-in-and-ask.\n7 steps · 4 min read read → (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/writing/claude-with-microsoft-365.html)\nVerified Salesforce credentials\nFour credentials verified through Salesforce's public credential record. The projects above provide separate implementation evidence.\n01 Salesforce Certified Agentforce Specialist — earned 18 August 2026 Verified\n02 Salesforce Certified Platform Administrator II — earned 10 August 2026 Verified\n03 Salesforce Certified Platform App Builder — earned 21 July 2026 Verified\n04 Salesforce Certified Platform Administrator — earned 23 June 2026 Verified\nView the public Salesforce credential record → (https://trailhead.salesforce.com/en/credentials/certification-detail-print/?searchString=/EMytG9drkgo/H4/0tgVITa/sw2U8vhbkvkc3jqlaJgauY5cCr+PvNo4YAw1Ki9f)\nReview the Salesforce User Lifecycle SOP → (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/salesforce-user-lifecycle-sop)\nHemayet Hossain · Sydney, Australia · github.com/hossainconsulting (https://github.com/hossainconsulting) · Reddit (agency) (https://www.reddit.com/user/hossainconsulting/) · Reddit (personal) (https://www.reddit.com/user/hemayetAI/) · all links (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/links) · Recruitment enquiries · Agency enquiries (https://hossainconsulting.com/) · Privacy (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/privacy) · Terms (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/terms)\nSalesforce and related marks are property of Salesforce, Inc.\n</page>\n\n<page url=\"https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/service-agent-patterns\" source=\"public/service-agent-patterns.html\">\nThe Meridian Engagement · Reference\nThe Service-Agent Pattern Library\nA public board offered 200 AI agent ideas for service businesses. Read across the columns instead of down them and it collapses into 25 patterns wearing eight different sets of nouns. Here they are, with the risk tier, the integration bill and the compliance exposure each one actually carries.\n← Portfolio (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/) Engagement repository (https://github.com/hossainconsulting/agentforce-meridian-care)\n200\nIdeas on the board\n25 cells across 8 service niches.\n25\nDistinct patterns\nWhat survives once the duplicates across niches collapse.\n8×\nRe-skinning factor\nTrades' warranty triage is Clinics' insurance chase with different nouns.\n6 / 15 / 4\nGreen / amber / red\nOnly four patterns can't let the model speak for itself.\nThe board presents itself as 200 opportunities: trades, cleaning, landscaping, clinics, salons, repair shops, agencies, consultants, 25 ideas each. Read column by column, that holds. Read row by row and the same skeleton repeats in nearly every one — missed-call capture, booking, reminders, review requests, invoice chase, handoff summaries, FAQ. Salons' waitlist filler is Trades' cancellation soft filler. Clinics' insurance document chase-up is Trades' warranty question triage.\nThe unit of reuse is the pattern, not the vertical. Build the triage-and-handoff engine once and eight columns become eight configurations, not eight builds.\n01\nThe risk tiers decide the architecture\nEvery pattern here carries a tier, and the tier is not a severity label for its own sake — it dictates where the answer is allowed to come from. This is the single most load-bearing distinction in the document.\nGreen 6\nA wrong answer costs a minute of someone's time. A reminder fires twice; an ETA is off by ten minutes.\nArchitectural ruleGeneration is fine. Ship it.\nAmber 15\nA wrong answer costs trust, or a booking. A misrouted enquiry, a bad summary, a review request sent to an angry customer.\nArchitectural ruleGeneration anchored on a retrieved fact, with a measured error rate.\nRed 4\nA wrong answer costs money, safety or a legal breach. Coverage statements, clinical urgency, pricing, payment claims, anything touching a health record.\nArchitectural ruleThe answer comes from a retrieved action, never the model. The model may only phrase what the action returned.\nThe red-tier rule is the generalised form of the constraint the Meridian build runs on: coverage answers come from Warranty_Entitlement__c.Is_Currently_Covered__c, a formula field the agent retrieves, not a judgement it generates. The same logic extends to any statement a customer could reasonably act on or hold you to. Under the Australian Consumer Law a claim about coverage, remedy, repair rights or price is a representation — an agent that improvises one is a compliance problem wearing a chat interface.\n02\nThe twenty-five patterns\nGrouped by lifecycle stage. The column that matters is the last one: in most of these the intelligence is a thin band and everything around it is deterministic code.\nStage 1\nCapture and qualify\n#Pattern & triggerThe AI's actual jobTier\n01\nMissed-call capture\nUnanswered inbound call → SMS within 60 seconds capturing intent\nAI's actual job\nParse the reply into an intent and a callback preference\nGreen\n02\nEnquiry triage\nInbound form, email or DM → classified and routed\nAI's actual job\nClassify intent, extract entities, pick a queue\nAmber\n03\nLead qualification\nNew enquiry → qualified, or politely declined\nAI's actual job\nJudge fit against service area, job type and budget signal\nAmber\n04\nUrgency triage\nAny inbound → emergency path or normal queue\nAI's actual job\nDetect the red flag — gas, water, electrical, clinical\nRed\n05\nQuote request intake\nFree text and photos → structured scope on a record\nAI's actual job\nExtract scope; describe what is in the photo\nAmber\nPattern 04 is red-tiered and people miss it. An agent that fails to recognise \"I can smell gas\" or a cardiac description is not a bad chatbot, it is an incident. Emergency detection should be keyword-and-rule first, model second, and it should over-escalate by design.\nStage 2\nBook and schedule\n#Pattern & triggerThe AI's actual jobTier\n06\nBooking agent\nQualified enquiry → slot written to the calendar\nAI's actual job\nTurn \"sometime Thursday arvo\" into a slot query\nAmber\n07\nAppointment reminder\nT−24h and T−2h → confirmation received\nAI's actual job\nAlmost none. A scheduled job with a template.\nGreen\n08\nReschedule handler\nInbound \"can we move it?\" → calendar updated\nAI's actual job\nInterpret a vague new time, then confirm it back\nGreen\n09\nCancellation backfill\nSlot freed → offered to the waitlist in priority order\nAI's actual job\nDraft the offer; rank who to ask first\nGreen\n10\nNo-show recovery\nMissed appointment → rebooked, or closed out\nAI's actual job\nTone. A message that reads as a telling-off loses the customer.\nAmber\n11\nRoute and ETA update\nTechnician running late → customer told before they notice\nAI's actual job\nNone. Geofence plus template.\nGreen\n12\nWaitlist manager\nCapacity opens → waitlist worked down\nAI's actual job\nRank by fit and recency\nGreen\nSix of the twelve patterns so far are green, and several need no model at all. That is the board's own best advice, and it is correct: start with follow-up, scheduling and reminders, then add AI only where language, judgment or triage genuinely appear. Most of the value in Stage 2 is a working queue, not a clever one.\nStage 3\nDeliver and hand off\n#Pattern & triggerThe AI's actual jobTier\n13\nVisit note summariser\nVoice memo or scrappy notes → structured record\nAI's actual job\nGenuine summarisation. The strongest fit on the whole board.\nAmber\n14\nPhoto evidence summary\nBefore and after images → written condition note\nAI's actual job\nVision description, tightly scoped\nAmber\n15\nCrew and clinician handoff\nShift or ownership change → receiving party briefed\nAI's actual job\nCompress a history into what the next person needs\nRedclinical\n16\nEscalation handoff\nAgent hits its limit → human takes over with the full transcript\nAI's actual job\nSummarise the conversation; never drop the raw transcript\nAmber\n17\nChecklist and SOP Q&A\n\"What's the procedure for X?\" → grounded answer\nAI's actual job\nRetrieval over the actual SOP, with a citation\nAmber\nPattern 16 is the one nobody builds and everybody needs. Every conversation needs a human escalation path, and the handoff must carry the transcript. The failure mode that destroys trust fastest is a customer made to explain their problem twice — most \"our chatbot was terrible\" stories are a missing pattern 16.\nStage 4\nClose and collect\n#Pattern & triggerThe AI's actual jobTier\n18\nInvoice reminder\nInvoice ages past terms → escalating reminder sequence\nAI's actual job\nTone laddering across the sequence\nAmber\n19\nPayment status assistant\n\"Has that been paid?\" → answer from the ledger\nAI's actual job\nPhrase a retrieved balance. Nothing else.\nRed\n20\nDocument collection\nMissing form, certificate or insurance doc → chased until received\nAI's actual job\nTrack what is outstanding; draft the chase\nAmber\nStage 5\nRetain and grow\n#Pattern & triggerThe AI's actual jobTier\n21\nSatisfaction check-in\nJob closed plus N days → sentiment captured\nAI's actual job\nRead sentiment from a free-text reply\nAmber\n22\nReview request\nPositive sentiment confirmed → review link sent\nAI's actual job\nGate on 21. Never ask an unhappy customer.\nAmber\n23\nComplaint triage\nNegative sentiment or explicit complaint → owner assigned, clock started\nAI's actual job\nClassify severity and route; never resolve autonomously\nRed\n24\nReactivation\nDormant customer, or recurring service due → re-engaged\nAI's actual job\nPersonalise on service history\nAmber\n25\nRenewal and upsell prompt\nContract or plan approaching end → offer made\nAI's actual job\nDraft the offer against actual usage\nAmber\nPatterns 21 and 22 must be built as one thing. A review request that fires without a sentiment gate is a machine for collecting one-star reviews at scale.\n03\nWhat the board doesn't cost in\nThe infographic's implicit promise is that the idea is the hard part. It isn't. Four costs sit between any cell on that grid and a working system — and the first of them is most of the build.\nIntegration surface\nThe agent is perhaps a tenth of the work. Auth, sync, retry, reconciliation and the sandbox you test it in are the rest. Grouped by what they touch, in the Australian market:\nSystem class | Australian examples | Patterns\nTelephony & SMS | Twilio, MessageMedia | 01, 07, 08, 10, 11, 18\nField service | ServiceM8, Jobber, simPRO, Tradify | 05, 06, 09, 12, 13, 14\nPractice management | Cliniko, Halaxy, Best Practice | 04, 06, 07, 10, 15\nBooking & rostering | Fresha, Timely, Square | 06, 08, 09, 12\nAccounting | Xero, MYOB | 18, 19, 20, 25\nCRM & case | Salesforce, HubSpot | 02, 03, 16, 21, 23, 24\nData readiness\nPattern 22 needs to know the job finished and went well. Pattern 01 needs call logs joined to customer records. Pattern 19 needs a ledger the agent can read. Most small operators cannot answer these questions today, and no amount of model quality fixes an unqueryable \"job complete\" flag.\nVolume economics\nA three-van plumbing business misses perhaps fifteen calls a week. At ten minutes recovered each that is two and a half hours — real, but small. The economics concentrate where volume does. Meridian, at 12,400 cases a month across 35 seats, sits on the right side of that line. The solo tradie in the board's hero image does not.\n\"Owned, not rented\"\nThe board's closing claim is positioning, not architecture. You own the orchestration logic, the prompts and the data. You rent the model API, the SMS gateway, the scheduling SaaS and the host. Worth saying plainly to any client who repeats the phrase back to you.\nThe measurement you skipped\nEvery pattern here implies a baseline you needed before you built. Meridian's is stark and typical: 71% CSAT, 9:40 average handle time, 28% repeat contact, and 0% self-service deflection. An improvement you cannot count is an improvement that gets switched off in month four.\n04\nThe Australian compliance layer\nRoughly a third of the board's cells carry an obligation the board never mentions. The exposure is not evenly spread — it clusters hard in the retention patterns and in the entire clinics column.\nRegime | Bites | The practical constraint\nSpam Act 2003 (Cth) | 21, 22, 24, 25 | Commercial electronic messages need consent, sender identification and a functional unsubscribe. Review requests, reactivation and upsell offers are commercial.\nSpam Act — transactional | 07, 08, 10, 11, 18, 20 | Messages about an existing booking or debt are generally factual rather than commercial. Still collect consent at booking; the line moves the moment an offer is attached.\nDo Not Call Register Act 2006 | outbound | Applies where a pattern places marketing calls rather than sending messages.\nPrivacy Act 1988 — APPs | all | APP 3 collection, APP 5 notification, APP 6 use and disclosure, APP 11 security. Conversation transcripts are personal information.\nPrivacy Act — health | 04, 06, 10, 15, 20 | Health service providers are covered regardless of turnover — the small-business exemption does not apply. State law stacks on top, e.g. the NSW Health Records and Information Privacy Act 2002.\nAustralian Consumer Law | 04, 05, 17, 19, 23 | Statements about coverage, remedy, repair rights or price are representations. A generated one is misleading-conduct risk.\nAdd to this a practice standard rather than a statute: disclose that the customer is talking to an AI, and make the escalation path visible in the first message. Not yet a general legal requirement in Australia, but it is where expectations are heading and it costs nothing.\nNot legal advice\nGeneral Australian context for a real build. Confirm with someone qualified before any of these patterns ship to customers.\n05\nThe map to Meridian\nMeridian Appliance Care is a fictional warranty administrator built as a six-week Agentforce engagement simulation — 35 contact-centre seats, 12,400 cases a month, Agentforce licensed after a board directive and never switched on. Here is which of the twenty-five it answers, and where.\n# | Pattern | Status | Component\n02 | Enquiry triage | Covered | Case record types + Meridian Case Routing assignment rule\n04 | Urgency triage | Partial | Tier-based, not content-based — Retailer VIP routes to Escalations\n16 | Escalation handoff | Designed | Week 3 agent, transcript-carrying handoff\n17 | Checklist & SOP Q&A | Planned | Agent FAQ topic\n19 | Coverage status assistant | Covered | The red-tier exemplar — Is_Currently_Covered__c formula, read by a retrieved action\n23 | Complaint triage | Partial | Resolution_Category__c captures the outcome, not severity routing\n13 | Visit note summariser | Out of scope | Case comments exist; no summarisation\n18, 20 | Invoice & document collection | Out of scope | No billing object in the data model\n21, 22 | Check-in & review request | Out of scope | No marketing consent model\nA single cell, done properly\nThe whole engagement is essentially pattern 19 plus 02, 16 and 17, built to a standard the board never describes: coverage as a computed fact, restricted picklists so an import can't invent a fourth SLA tier, and a first-response clock — 48, 24 and 8 business hours — running on Australia/Sydney business hours, Mon–Sat 8:00–18:00, so a VIP breach can't happen overnight while nobody is working. The board sells breadth. The value is depth.\nThe gaps are deliberate\nPatterns 21–25 need a marketing consent model Meridian doesn't have; 18–20 need a billing integration outside the six-week scope. Naming the cells you chose not to build, and why, is what separates a scoped engagement from an unfinished one.\n06\nThe pick-one test\nWhen a client points at the grid and asks which cell to build first, four criteria, in this order.\n01Frequency\nDoes it happen daily? Weekly is too rare to learn from.\n02Blast radius\nGreen tier only for a first build. Never start on a red cell.\n03Baseline\nCan you measure today's performance before you build? If not, you cannot prove the thing worked.\n04Single integration\nOne system to write to. Two systems triples the timeline.\nScored that way, the board's own \"best places to start\" box turns out to be right: missed-call follow-up, bookings and reminders, enquiry qualification, review follow-up. Patterns 01, 06, 07 and 21/22 — two green, two amber, all with an obvious baseline.\nThe Meridian corollary, and the rule the engagement is built on: define how you count a deflected conversation, build the report, then launch. Not the other way around.\nDerived from a public marketing infographic, \"200 Business Service Agent Ideas for Service Businesses\" (AI Matt, @aimattant), analysed 4 September 2026.\nMeridian Appliance Care Pty Ltd is a fictional company. This page documents a self-directed Agentforce Specialist simulation; no real customer data appears anywhere in it.\nHemayet Hossain · Sydney, Australia · github.com/hossainconsulting (https://github.com/hossainconsulting) · Reddit (agency) (https://www.reddit.com/user/hossainconsulting/) · Reddit (personal) (https://www.reddit.com/user/hemayetAI/)\n</page>\n\n<page url=\"https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/writing/claude-with-microsoft-365\" source=\"public/writing/claude-with-microsoft-365.html\">\n← Hemayet Hossain (https://portfolio.hossainconsulting.com/)\nWriting · AI in practice\nHow to use Claude with Microsoft 365\nMost people use Microsoft 365 every day. Almost none of them use Claude with it. Here are seven places it earns its keep.\nThe whole workflow on one page — the seven steps below, in order.\nThe workflow: steps across Microsoft 365\n01\nOutlook\nManage emails, save time, and communicate with clarity.\nSummarise long email threads in seconds\nExtract key points, action items and deadlines\nDraft, rewrite or improve email responses\nAdjust tone: professional, friendly, concise\nExample Summarise a 15-email thread, pull out the action items, and draft a concise reply that moves the conversation forward.\n02\nWord\nCreate, edit and refine documents faster.\nDraft first versions from an outline or prompt\nRewrite and strengthen content for clarity\nImprove structure, flow and tone\nShorten, expand or simplify sections\nExample Turn rough notes into a polished document, improve the clarity, and get it ready to share.\n03\nExcel\nWork with data, find insights, and make better decisions.\nExplain what your data means in plain language\nIdentify trends, patterns and outliers\nHelp with formulas, functions and calculations\nCreate summaries, charts and recommendations\nExample Explain last quarter’s sales data, spot the trends, and suggest next steps to improve performance.\n04\nPowerPoint\nTurn ideas into engaging presentations.\nTurn content or documents into slide outlines\nCreate compelling titles, headings and copy\nSuggest visuals, layouts and slide flow\nGenerate speaker notes and key takeaways\nExample Create a 10-slide deck outline, write the key messages, and generate the speaker notes for your presentation.\n05\nOneNote\nCapture ideas, organise notes, and build knowledge.\nTurn messy notes into clear, organised summaries\nExtract action items, decisions and next steps\nCreate checklists, templates and frameworks\nConnect ideas and build knowledge bases\nExample Convert meeting notes into action items, assign owners, and organise them by project.\n06\nTeams\nCollaborate effectively and stay aligned.\nSummarise meeting transcripts and chats\nHighlight decisions, risks and action items\nDraft follow-up messages and announcements\nCreate agendas and status updates\nExample Summarise a Teams meeting, list the key decisions and action items, and draft a follow-up message for the team.\n07\nOneDrive\nFind files, organise content, and surface insights.\nFind the right files and information fast\nSummarise documents across multiple files\nCompare versions and pull key takeaways\nOrganise and tag content for easy access\nExample Find the relevant project files, summarise the key documents, and create a one-page executive summary.\nHow the connection actually works\nNone of the above requires Microsoft to ship anything. Some of it runs through Claude’s connectors, which read your mail, calendar and files directly; the rest is paste-in-and-ask, which costs nothing to try and works from any plan.\nWhich connectors are available to you depends on your Claude plan and on what your Microsoft 365 administrator permits, so check the connector list in your own account before designing a workflow around one.\nThe part worth keeping\nThe pattern under all seven steps is the same: Claude is good at the reading, the drafting and the summarising, and you stay responsible for the judgement. Ask it to explain the data, not to decide what to do about it. Ask it to draft the reply, not to send it.\nThat division is also what makes the output safe to put in front of a client — which is the standard the rest of this site is built to.\nHemayet Hossain · Sydney, Australia · github.com/hossainconsulting (https://github.com/hossainconsulting) · Reddit (agency) (https://www.reddit.com/user/hossainconsulting/) · Reddit (personal) (https://www.reddit.com/user/hemayetAI/)\nMicrosoft, Microsoft 365, Outlook, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, OneNote, Teams and OneDrive are trademarks of Microsoft Corporation. Claude is a trademark of Anthropic, PBC. This page is not affiliated with or endorsed by either.\n</page>";
